//! lotus/tremolo.rye — ALES74: the tremolo — the triangle LFO turned on AMPLITUDE, not delay. The chorus (ALES71), the vibrato (ALES72),
//! and the flanger (ALES73) all point the triangle LFO at ONE target — the delay: a mixed swinging copy, that copy heard alone, that copy fed
//! back. The LFO driver `triangle_delay_at` is a GENERAL scaled triangle, though — named for its first use, it knows nothing of delay. Point it
//! at the sample's own amplitude instead, and a steady note gently rises and falls in loudness: that is the TREMOLO, the oldest amplitude
//! effect there is, and the modulation family's first rung on a genuinely new target.
//!
//! The crux is that THE SAME TRIANGLE THAT DROVE THE DELAY DRIVES THE GAIN — no new LFO, no new arithmetic, only a new target. Over one period
//! P (a positive multiple of four, q = P/4) with phase p = k % P, the scaled gain is g(k) = centre_scaled + triangle(depth_scaled, P, k), read
//! in units of glide_scale (256 = unity); then y[i] = saturate(x[i]·g(i)/glide_scale). Because every output sample depends only on ITS OWN
//! input sample — never a neighbour — the tremolo writes IN PLACE WITH NO SNAPSHOT: reading x[i] and writing y[i] at the same index touches no
//! sample another step needs. The vibrato had to freeze the dry prefix precisely because its read moved backward through samples it was
//! overwriting; the tremolo's read never moves, so the whole snapshot machinery falls away — this is the thinnest rung in the modulation family.
//!
//! The gain is held to an ATTENUATION — 0 ≤ g(k) ≤ glide_scale (top at or below unity, floor at or above silence) — so x·g/scale is never
//! louder than x itself and so ALREADY fits the i16 rail: the write goes through the one true saturate for family uniformity, yet it never
//! actually clips (a full-scale sample at unity passes through byte-for-byte, at the trough falls to silence, between traces the triangle).
//! There is no BadDelay and no BadLevel — the tremolo has no delay line to overrun and no dry/wet mix to level; the gain IS the level.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES74.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260814-201204_fill-ales74-lotus-tremolo-amplitude-modulation.md
//! Stands on: lotus/chorus.rye (ALES71) — the triangle-LFO driver `triangle_delay_at` this reuses, read as a gain; lotus/glide.rye (ALES70) —
//! `glide_scale`, the fixed-point unit (256 = unity gain); lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/max, the one true saturate.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM in one clip, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the gain is counted in scaled units (256 = unity) and the period in samples, not hertz (the
//! real-time twin follows as echo_ms and multitap_ms did). No delay line, no snapshot, no lookahead. The triangle and the multiply run in i64
//! and the write saturates once (a no-op on the attenuated voice), so nothing on the path can overflow.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/tremolo.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const glide = @import("glide.rye");
const chorus = @import("chorus.rye");

pub const tremolo_version = "20260814.201204";

/// The tremolo's faults, one consistent name each: a period that is not a positive multiple of four (the triangle's quarter anchors need P/4
/// whole) or wider than max_clip, a swing whose floor (centre − depth) falls below silence (a negative gain would be a sign flip, a different
/// effect), a swing whose top (centre + depth) rises above unity (glide_scale — the tremolo attenuates, it does not boost), and a span outside
/// the samples. Each refused before any write. There is NO BadDelay and NO BadLevel — the tremolo has no delay line and no mix level; the gain
/// IS the level.
pub const TremoloError = error{
    /// A period that is not a positive multiple of four, or wider than max_clip — refused, no write.
    BadPeriod,
    /// A negative depth, or a swing whose floor (centre − depth) falls below silence (a negative gain) — refused, no write.
    BadDepth,
    /// A swing whose top (centre + depth) rises above unity (glide_scale) — the tremolo attenuates, never boosts — refused, no write.
    BadGain,
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Apply a tremolo over `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip`: a triangle LFO of `depth_scaled` round `centre_scaled` over
/// `period` samples drives the scaled GAIN (ALES71's `triangle_delay_at`, read as a gain in units of glide_scale), and each sample is scaled
/// by that gain — y[i] = saturate(x[i]·g(i)/glide_scale). No delay line and no snapshot: every output depends only on its own input sample, so
/// the in-place read-then-write at one index touches nothing another step needs. The gain is an attenuation (0 ≤ g ≤ glide_scale), so the
/// write saturates once yet never clips — the tremolo voice is no louder than the dry it scales, and so already fits the i16 rail. Refuses
/// BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadGain, and BadRange before any write; on refusal the clip is untouched. Values only — never a length.
pub fn tremolo(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    centre_scaled: i64,
    depth_scaled: i64,
    period: u32,
) TremoloError!void {
    // invariant: the period is a positive multiple of four (the triangle's quarter anchors need P/4 whole) and no wider than the widest
    // clip, so the LFO is bounded. Checked at the edge before any read.
    if (period == 0 or period % 4 != 0) return error.BadPeriod;
    if (period > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadPeriod;
    // invariant: the depth is non-negative and the swing's floor stays at or above silence — a negative gain would flip the sample's sign,
    // a different effect entirely, not a tremolo.
    if (depth_scaled < 0) return error.BadDepth;
    if (centre_scaled - depth_scaled < 0) return error.BadDepth;
    // invariant: the swing's top stays at or below unity — the tremolo attenuates, it does not boost; a gain above glide_scale could push a
    // loud sample past the rail. This is what keeps the write's saturate a proven no-op.
    if (centre_scaled + depth_scaled > glide.glide_scale) return error.BadGain;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder).
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const scale: i64 = glide.glide_scale;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        // ALES71's `triangle_delay_at` is a general scaled triangle — here it drives the GAIN, not a delay. g in [centre − depth, centre + depth].
        const g: i64 = chorus.triangle_delay_at(centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period, k);
        // invariant: the gain never leaves the validated attenuation window, so it stays between silence and unity.
        assert(g >= 0 and g <= scale);
        const dry: i64 = @as(i64, clip.buf[i]);
        // invariant: the sample read is a legal i16 — it is audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(dry >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and dry <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        // The gained voice: dry scaled by g/scale. |dry| ≤ 32768 and g ≤ scale, so the product fits i64 and the quotient's magnitude is no
        // larger than |dry|. g = scale returns dry exactly; g = 0 returns silence; between, a fraction of the dry.
        const gained: i64 = @divTrunc(dry * g, scale);
        // invariant: the tremolo voice is an attenuation of an in-range sample, so it ALREADY fits the i16 rail — the saturate below is a
        // documented no-op, never an actual clip. This is the whole reason the amplitude write cannot overflow.
        assert(gained >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and gained <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        clip.buf[i] = timeline.saturate(gained);
    }
    // invariant: a tremolo changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: A STEADY SIGNAL TRACES THE TRIANGLE GAIN (the family recognition, proven sample-by-sample against the driver); a hand-computed
//     whole-anchor case matches exactly; a zero-depth tremolo is a constant-gain fader (hand-computed); unity passthrough is exact even at the
//     rail (the saturate no-op); the tremolo voice never exceeds the dry rail; and each fault refuses by name, the clip untouched -----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const scale = glide.glide_scale;

    // 1. A STEADY SIGNAL TRACES THE TRIANGLE GAIN — the family recognition. A DC signal of value A under the tremolo yields
    //    y[k] == A·g(k)/scale at every sample, matched directly against ALES71's driver. The LFO drives amplitude the same way it drove delay.
    {
        const a: i16 = 12000;
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ a, a, a, a, a, a, a, a });
        const centre: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2);
        const depth: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2);
        try tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, centre, depth, 8);
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < clip.len) : (k += 1) {
            const g = chorus.triangle_delay_at(centre, depth, 8, k);
            const want: i16 = timeline.saturate(@divTrunc(@as(i64, a) * g, scale));
            if (clip.samples()[k] != want) return error.DcNotTriangleGain;
        }
    }

    // 2. A HAND-COMPUTED WHOLE-ANCHOR CASE — centre scale/2, depth scale/2, period 4 (q = 1): the gain traces the four anchors
    //    centre → top → centre → bottom → centre = ½ → 1 → ½ → 0 → ½ → 1. On a steady 1000:
    //      k:      0    1     2   3    4    5
    //      g(k):   128  256   128 0    128  256   (½, 1, ½, 0, ½, 1 of scale=256)
    //      y[k]: 1000·g/256 =  500 1000 500 0 500 1000
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), @divTrunc(scale, 2), 4);
        const want = [_]i16{ 500, 1000, 500, 0, 500, 1000 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.HandAnchorWrong;
    }

    // 3. A ZERO-DEPTH TREMOLO IS A CONSTANT-GAIN FADER — depth == 0, the LFO at rest, a fixed gain of `centre`: the output is a plain scaling
    //    x·centre/scale, equal to a fader set once. centre = scale/2 halves every sample.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 300, 400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), 0, 4);
        const want = [_]i16{ 50, 100, 150, 200 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.ZeroDepthNotFader;
    }

    // 4. UNITY PASSTHROUGH IS EXACT — centre = scale, depth = 0: the gain is one, so every sample passes through byte-for-byte, including
    //    sample_min at the very edge of the rail. This proves the write's saturate is a documented no-op.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, -1, 0, 12345, timeline.sample_max };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, scale, 0, 4);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.UnityNotPassthrough;
    }

    // 5. THE TREMOLO VOICE NEVER EXCEEDS THE DRY RAIL — a loud steady signal under a full swing stays within [sample_min, sample_max] and
    //    never wraps: an attenuation of an in-range sample is in range. A steady -30000 under centre ½, depth ½ traces the triangle downward
    //    from -15000, never past the rail.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ -30000, -30000, -30000, -30000 });
        try tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), @divTrunc(scale, 2), 4);
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < clip.len) : (k += 1) {
            const v = clip.samples()[k];
            if (v < timeline.sample_min or v > timeline.sample_max) return error.TremoloExceedsRail;
        }
    }

    // 6a. A PERIOD not a positive multiple of four refuses BadPeriod; zero refuses BadPeriod; a period wider than max_clip refuses BadPeriod —
    //     before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), 0, 6)) |_| return error.OddPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongOddPeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedOddPeriodMutated;
        if (tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), 0, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongZeroPeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedZeroPeriodMutated;
        const wide: u32 = timeline.max_clip + 4;
        if (tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), 0, wide)) |_| return error.WidePeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongWidePeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedWidePeriodMutated;
    }

    // 6b. A DEPTH that swings below silence refuses BadDepth; a negative depth refuses BadDepth; a swing top past unity refuses BadGain — each
    //     before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        // centre ½·scale, depth ¾·scale → floor = centre − depth < 0
        if (tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), @divTrunc(3 * scale, 4), 4)) |_| return error.LowFloorSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDepth) return error.WrongLowFloor;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedLowFloorMutated;
        // negative depth
        if (tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), -1, 4)) |_| return error.NegDepthSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDepth) return error.WrongNegDepth;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedNegDepthMutated;
        // centre unity, depth ½·scale → top = centre + depth > glide_scale
        if (tremolo(&clip, 0, clip.len, scale, @divTrunc(scale, 2), 4)) |_| return error.HighTopSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongHighTop;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedHighTopMutated;
    }

    // 6c. AN OUT-OF-RANGE SPAN refuses BadRange before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (tremolo(&clip, 2, 5, @divTrunc(scale, 2), 0, 4)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-tremolo: the TREMOLO — the triangle LFO turned on AMPLITUDE, not delay, the modulation family's first rung on a new " ++
        "target and its thinnest. It reuses ALES71's triangle_delay_at verbatim, reading its result as a GAIN in units of glide_scale " ++
        "(256 = unity) rather than a delay: y[i] = saturate(x[i]·g(i)/glide_scale). Because every output depends only on its own input " ++
        "sample, the tremolo writes IN PLACE WITH NO SNAPSHOT — the vibrato's frozen-prefix machinery falls away. So a STEADY SIGNAL TRACES " ++
        "THE TRIANGLE GAIN, proven sample-by-sample against the driver; a hand-computed whole-anchor case (½ → 1 → ½ → 0) matches exactly; a " ++
        "zero-depth tremolo is a constant-gain fader; unity passthrough is exact even at sample_min (so the write's saturate is a proven " ++
        "no-op); the tremolo voice never exceeds the dry rail (an attenuation of an in-range sample is in range); and a period not a positive " ++
        "multiple of four refuses BadPeriod, a swing below silence or a negative depth BadDepth, a swing top past unity BadGain, an " ++
        "out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched — there is no BadDelay and no BadLevel, the tremolo has no delay line " ++
        "and no mix level, the gain IS the level; no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate. Auto-pan " ++
        "(the same LFO on the stereo field) and the ring modulator (a faster carrier on the same gain) are the family's next rungs on this " ++
        "new target\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
